# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
.next/
out/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Generated on demand (created by scripts/code-templates/orchestrator.js)
generated/

# Your brand answers — hand-authored, project-specific, not Loom's look.
# Copy spec/answers.example.json → spec/answers.json and edit.
spec/answers.json

# ...and what `npm run configs` generates from them. The rule above covers the
# pipeline's input; this covers its output, which is the half that actually reached
# master twice with the wrong brand. Generators prefer this set and fall back to the
# committed spec/config/base/, so a fresh clone builds Loom's own look with no answers
# file at all. Together these two rules make the claim the first one used to make alone:
# generating your brand never dirties the Loom working tree.
spec/config/local/

# The playground's token substrate, regenerated by its own predev/prebuild hook from
# whichever config set is active. It is deliberately NOT committed: the playground is a
# live reflection of your tokens — edit spec/config/local/, re-run, watch the ramps and
# sizing move — so a tracked copy would have to be both canonical (to be reviewable) and
# yours (to be useful). It cannot be both. Untracked, `./setup.sh catalog-playground`
# writing your brand into it is the feature rather than a leak into a committed file.
catalog-playground/src/tokens.css
catalog-playground/src/loom.css
catalog-playground/src/loom.components.css
catalog-playground/src/app/preview/
catalog-playground/src/loom.tailwind.css

# Logs / env / OS
*.log
.env*
.DS_Store
